// Why each recommended brand and each pick is in the guide. Plain sentences for visitors, one per key.
// WHY is keyed by the brand's exact name in TIERS (app.js) and is shown on the brand's row and under the map.
// PICK_WHY is keyed by the pick's key in PICKS (app.js) and is shown on the pick's card.
// Facts were checked against makers' sites, the watch press and owner reviews in November 2026.
const WHY = {
"Seagull":"Tianjin Sea-Gull is one of the world's biggest movement makers, and it sells watches under its own name too. Its best-known watch is the 1963 Chronograph, a reissue of a watch made for the Chinese air force, with a hand-wound column-wheel chronograph movement on show for a few hundred dollars. Buy from a known importer, as copies are common.",
"Laco":"A Pforzheim maker best known for German pilot's watches; it was one of the original makers of the big wartime Flieger watches. It now makes pilot, navy and everyday watches at fair prices, including hand-wound models with decorated Unitas-based movements on show. Prices from US dealers are higher than Laco's own euro prices.",
"Tutima":"A Glashütte maker with roots in the 1920s, known for military pilot's chronographs and, more recently, its own movements built the traditional Glashütte way. The Patria shows its in-house hand-wound Calibre 617 through the back. Dealer prices vary a lot, so shop around.",
"Casio and G-Shock":"The square G-Shock has been made since 1983, and the GW-M5610U ($165) adds a solar cell and radio time-setting: about 10 months on a full charge, 200 m water resistance, and it sets itself to the second. Owners report it surviving falls of two storeys. The complaints are a mineral-glass crystal and confusing model names in the US.",
"Orient":"The Bambino gives you an automatic movement made by Orient itself, which you can also wind by hand, behind a see-through back, for $295–460. Orient belongs to Seiko Epson, the company behind Epson printers. Reviewers call it the best-value mechanical dress watch. Owners complain that the domed mineral crystal and shiny dial are hard to read, and that it is only rated to 30 m.",
"Seiko":"Japan's first wristwatch (1913) and first dive watch (1965) were Seikos, and Seiko still makes its own movements and even the alloy for its springs. The Presage Cocktail Time ($450–475) has a dial reviewers rate as good enough for a $2,000 watch. The complaints: quality control lets through the odd misaligned part, and the crystal is mineral glass, not sapphire.",
"Zelos":"One engineer in Singapore designs every watch, in materials rarely seen at this price: bronze, meteorite and Timascus, a patterned titanium. The Mako diver gives you 300 m, a sapphire crystal and a ceramic bezel for $449. The catches: almost everything sells out (17 of 578 listings were in stock when checked), and owner reviews are mixed on bracelet finishing.",
"Brew":"An industrial designer's watches styled after espresso machines; the Metric chronograph's dial even marks the 25–35 seconds it takes to pull a shot. Sapphire crystal and a clasp with fine adjustment for $475. Every watch costs under $500. Note that the chronograph is quartz with a mechanical stopwatch module, it is only rated to 50 m, and some versions don't glow in the dark.",
"Helm":"A 300 m diver certified to the ISO dive-watch standard, with every watch water-tested at 125% of its rating, a sapphire crystal and a Japanese automatic movement, for $285. Owners say it feels far dearer than it is. The catches: you join a waiting list (one owner waited 20 months), it weighs 235 g on the bracelet, and the warranty is one year.",
"Mr Jones Watches":"Artists design the dials, which are printed by hand in the brand's London workshop. In 'A perfectly useless afternoon' a swimmer's leg shows the hour and a duck shows the minutes. Most designs are about $325, quartz, with a sapphire crystal and cheap repairs for life. Some designs give only a rough time, and the polished case scratches easily.",
"Smiths":"A revived British name sold by Time Factors, a small UK business. The 36 mm Everest gives you a sapphire crystal, 100 m water resistance and magnetism resistance for £345. Owners praise the build for the money. Be aware that it copies the Rolex Explorer closely, it is only sold in timed Sunday releases that sell out, and US buyers pay import duty.",
"Scurfa":"Made by Paul Scurfield, a commercial saturation diver of over 30 years, who tested the valve that lets helium escape on his own dives to 154 m. The Diver One is a 500 m quartz diver with a sapphire crystal; aBlogtoWatch's reviewer used one at work as a diver. It is a two-person business: stock comes in batches, and support can be slow.",
"Vostok":"A Russian dive watch with its own automatic movement and clever old engineering, such as a domed crystal that flexes and seals tighter under pressure, for $63–100. Owners love the history and how easy it is to customise. It is rough by modern standards: it gains or loses up to a minute a day, the lume is weak and the bracelet is poor. It ships from Russia, which can take two months.",
"Tissot":"Tissot's Powermatic 80 runs 80 hours, over three days off the wrist, and in many models has a magnet-resistant silicon hairspring. It's made by ETA, the Swatch Group's movement maker. The PRX, a 1978 design, costs $850 with a sapphire crystal and 100 m water resistance. Owners' main complaints are weak lume and clasps without fine adjustment, and the warranty is two years.",
"Hamilton":"Hamilton made field watches for the US military, and the Khaki Field Mechanical recreates its 1960s issue watch: hand-wound, 80 hours, $675. The automatics share Tissot's 80-hour Swatch Group movement family. Reviewers praise the build and finishing for the price. The complaints are lume that doesn't cover the numerals on the Expedition, and 50 m water resistance on the Mechanical.",
"Certina":"In the guide for its one picked watch, not the whole brand. The DS Action Diver 38 Titanium is certified to the ISO dive-watch standard, rated to 300 m, about half the weight of steel, and runs 80 hours on a magnet-resistant movement, for about $1,100. DS, 'Double Security', is Certina's shock- and water-resistance design from 1959. The complaints: the new version is thicker at 13.2 mm, and the clasp has no tool-free fine adjustment.",
"Mido":"In the guide for its picked watches, not the whole brand. The Ocean Star GMT, with an 80-hour reserve, is one of the cheapest Swiss watches with a true second time zone. The Ocean Star 39 is a 200 m diver only 10.5 mm thick, with a 72-hour movement and a magnet-resistant hairspring, which reviewers single out. Note that it is now $1,250 on Mido's US site and the bezel insert is aluminium, not ceramic. Owners mention a thick clasp and lume that fades fast.",
"Squale":"Squale has made professional dive watches since 1959 and made dive cases for other Swiss brands. Record-setting freedivers Enzo Maiorca and Jacques Mayol wore them. The 1521 keeps its 1960s design, rated to 500 m, with a Swiss Sellita movement and a sapphire crystal for about $1,265. Long-term owners compare it to a Tudor at a third of the price; they complain the recessed crown is hard to grip and the watch is heavy.",
"DOXA":"DOXA developed its 1967 Sub 300 with Jacques Cousteau's team, chose orange after underwater visibility tests, and supplied Swiss Army divers. The Sub 200 ($1,250–1,290) is the way into that history: 200 m, a sapphire crystal and eight dial colours. Note that the famous Sub 300 costs about $2,850, the Sub 200's reserve is only 38 hours, and owners of older models found the lume weak.",
"Farer":"British-designed and made by Swiss partners, with colour used better than almost anyone. The Resolute III ($1,245) has a top-grade 68-hour Swiss movement, a domed sapphire crystal and a sapphire back. The movement has a five-year guarantee (the case and crystal aren't covered). Some owners say the short lugs make it wear small and find it pricey for a small brand.",
"Studio Underd0g":"A small British brand known for playful, fruit-coloured dials. The 01SERIES chronograph ($850) is a hand-wound mechanical stopwatch with a column wheel, rare at this price, regulated in Britain, and every watch is assembled there by one named person. The complaints: it is sold in batches with long waits, the Chinese Seagull movement is only regulated to −10/+15 seconds a day, and it is rated to 50 m.",
"Stowa":"A German maker founded in 1927 that has made Bauhaus-style watches since 1937, building each watch to order in Pforzheim with a top-grade Swiss movement and a sapphire crystal. Its Antea won Germany's Goldene Unruh watch award in 2005. Note that it is sold direct in euros, so US buyers pay shipping and import duty on top, and the Antea is only rated to 50 m.",
"Junghans":"The Max Bill, drawn in 1961 by the Bauhaus-trained artist Max Bill, is one of the best-known watch designs, and Junghans still makes it in Germany. The automatic has a domed sapphire crystal in a 38 mm, 10 mm case for $1,670. You are paying for the design: the Swiss-based movement runs 38 hours and it is rated to 50 m.",
"Baltic":"A French brand founded in 2016, assembled and adjusted in France and sold direct, which keeps prices low. The Aquascaphe is a 200 m diver with a double-domed sapphire crystal and a sapphire bezel insert, from about €600. Owners love the vintage proportions. The complaints are the accuracy of its Japanese Miyota movement (one owner reports +30 seconds a day) and rough finishing in places.",
"Serica":"A Paris brand founded in 2019 whose every watch is a COSC-certified chronometer, which is rare at this price. The Field Chronometer is rated to 200 m in a case only 10.4 mm thick, for $1,290–1,390. Its dive watch was developed with an association of French Navy mine-clearance divers. About 70 owner reviews on Dialicious average 4.6 out of 5; the main complaint is that models are often sold out.",
"Furlan Marri":"Won the Geneva watchmaking Grand Prix's (GPHG) prize for the best new brand in 2021, seven months after launching on Kickstarter. The 38 mm chronographs cost about $695 with domed hands, engraved pushers and a sapphire crystal. Note that these use a Seiko quartz movement with a mechanical stopwatch, they are rated to 50 m, and some critics call the design too close to a Patek Philippe.",
"Maen":"Founded in Stockholm in 2017 by two Dutchmen and sold mostly direct, in batches that often sell out and go to pre-order. The Manhattan line has a 1970s-style integrated bracelet: the 39 Ultra-Thin is 6.9 mm thick with a Swiss hand-wound La Joux-Perret movement for about $1,350, and the 40 has an automatic. Reviewers like the dials and finishing for the money. The design leans heavily on famous luxury sports watches, the clasp has no fine adjustment, and returns are only accepted unworn and still sealed.",
"Bravur":"Started by two Swedish friends who raced mountain bikes, Bravur assembles watches in Båstad with Swiss movements, themed around the Grand Tours and classic cycling teams. Fratello and Worn & Wound like the colourful, well-made designs. Every model is 37 to 38 mm, small for a big wrist.",
"Elka Watch Co.":"Hakim El Kadiri revived the old Elka name in Neuchâtel in 2022, drawing on 1960s stopwatch and flight-instrument designs, and its X02 was nominated for the Geneva watchmaking Grand Prix (GPHG). The 40.8 mm X-Series uses a better-grade La Joux-Perret automatic. The brand is young and its newer models are a small 36 mm.",
"Lebois & Co":"A 1930s Swiss brand revived by the Dutch Van Wijlick family, who also run Airain. The Heritage Chronograph is a faithful 1940s reissue with a hand-wound La Joux-Perret column-wheel movement, the kind enthusiasts love, for about $3,200. It's 39 mm, and some coverage of it is sponsored.",
"Gorilla":"Founded in Neuchâtel in 2016 by two former Audemars Piguet designers, Gorilla makes loud, car-inspired octagonal watches in forged carbon, ceramic and titanium for about $1,200. Reviewers say the cases feel far above the price, and the 44 mm Fastback wears big. The core models are assembled in Asia with a basic Japanese movement.",
"Aera":"Launched in 2022 by Jas Minhas and Olof Larsson, Aera makes pared-back pilot, diver, field and chronograph watches with Swiss movements, sold direct and through Mr Porter. The 43 mm P-1 Pilot (about 50 mm lug to lug) suits a big wrist. Prices have risen quickly since launch, and some buyers grumble about its limited-edition marketing.",
"Ophion":"Miguel Morales Ribas's small Spanish brand makes Breguet- and pocket-watch-inspired watches with five-day hand-wound movements it reworks itself, with hand-hammered bridges and big polished bevels on show through the back. Worn & Wound called the OPH 786 one of the most genuinely interesting watches it had tried. The newest coverage is from 2023, so check current stock and prices.",
"Tourby":"A small workshop in Hagen, Germany, that sells direct and builds classic marine and dress watches around the big Unitas hand-wound movement. Its own Kaliber 74.3 swaps the plain bridges for a guilloché Glashütte-style three-quarter plate, on show through a sapphire back of a 43 mm case, from about €1,200. The cases are large and heavy because the movement began life in pocket watches.",
"Horologically Unique":"A Singapore brand that appeared in 2025 and made Worn & Wound's list of the year's best new brands. The HU-01 is a slim hand-wound dress watch after the Patek Calatrava 96, and the HU-02 brings back an Art Deco wandering jump-hour display. Everything is 34 to 38 mm, small for a big wrist.",
"Valérarch":"Founded in Seoul in 2022, Valérarch makes one watch, the Serenity 39, designed in Korea, made in Switzerland and inspired by Gothic arches. Monochrome praised the build quality. It's a lot of money for a one-model brand with a 39 mm automatic.",
"Ocean To Orbit":"A Melbourne brand that makes go-anywhere tool watches and tests them on real expeditions; its Lhotse was worn daily in the Himalayas. The 40 mm titanium Himalayan Project has a Swiss automatic. The movements are plain and nothing is over 40 mm.",
"Nectere":"A small Hong Kong brand whose asymmetric, lugless Order From Chaos case made the Geneva watchmaking Grand Prix (GPHG) list. Both main models are very thin and hand-wound, from $475. The watches are small, the movements are plain Chinese ones, and the brand is new.",
"Selten":"Leonardo Tsai's Hong Kong brand puts true grand feu enamel dials, fired in eight layers at 800°C, on a 39 mm watch with a slim Swiss automatic for about $1,900 to $2,900. Enamel like this usually costs many thousands more, and Worn & Wound, Fratello and Monochrome have all praised it. Runs are tiny and the watches are small.",
"Pitzmann":"A Seoul brand whose curved, bevelled cases follow giwa, the tiles on traditional Korean roofs. Time+Tide called it Korea's best value: the Pitzmann II is $1,099 with a Swiss movement and a skeletonised rotor. Everything is about 40 mm and automatic.",
"Beaufort":"A New Zealand brand launched on Kickstarter in 2019 and named after a Second World War torpedo bomber. It now makes a hand-wound chronograph, a 1970s-style diver and a GMT at fair prices, and Fratello and Worn & Wound like the sharp cases and dressy dials. The watches are 39 to 40 mm, and the hand-wound Pulsatimer has a solid back.",
"Feynman":"Founded in Singapore in 2018 by Lim Yong Keong and named after his son, who was named after the physicist. It builds slim dress watches around a top-grade Swiss Peseux 7001 hand-wound movement that it decorates, for about $1,200, and reviewers say the movement is about the best you can get at the price. Everything is about 39 mm, and many models are small runs.",
"Locke & King":"Ryan Moran's small Hamilton, Ontario brand, founded in 2020 and named after a local street corner, with vintage-style watches named after city streets. One blog rates its customer service the best of any microbrand. It uses budget Japanese movements and has little coverage outside Canada.",
"Hemel":"Designer Marvin Menke's New York brand, which started with Kickstarter field watches and now makes affordable takes on historic military and aviation watches. The 42 mm HFT20 chronograph comes as a $449 mecha-quartz or a $999 automatic. Reviewers say it avoids clichés; the movements are budget Japanese ones.",
"Ocean Crawler":"Christian Champion's Rochester, New York brand, founded in 2017, making tough, very bright dive watches with Swiss movements and a five-year warranty. The Core Diver (44 mm, about 50 mm lug to lug, 600 m, $999) suits a broad wrist. The designs are loud, there are frequent flash-sale limited editions, and one owner found service polite but slow.",
"Camp Watches":"Industrial designer Justin Jakobson's one-watch brand from Laurel Canyon. The Fieldtimer is a very slim hand-wound field watch with a 15-layer lacquer dial, its own typeface and a Sellita movement with a custom bridge on show through the back, for $900. It only comes in 39 mm and in runs of about 100 per colour.",
"Enchant":"A young Calgary brand whose Canadian Nord borrows from old railway pocket watches: small seconds, leaf hands and a railway minute track, assembled in Canada. A big 44 mm case holds a Swiss hand-wound pocket-watch movement on show through a sapphire back. It's costly at about £3,300 and has had little independent review.",
"Tessé":"Designer Thomas Brissiaud's young Toronto brand. Its Michel GMT is a 1970s-style cushion-case travel watch, designed in Canada and assembled in La Chaux-de-Fonds, Switzerland, for about $1,560. Reviewers like its slim proportions and bright dials. It's new and little known, and at 40 mm it isn't big.",
"DuFrane":"A small Austin brand, ten years old in 2026, whose watches are named after Austin places and assembled by hand there with Swiss movements. Buyers keep praising the owner for personal service. For a big wrist, the Bergstrom Air pilot's watch (41.5 mm, about 50 mm lug to lug, $799) is the one to try; most other models wear smaller, and the hand-wound Westlake is a slim 36 mm.",
"Halios":"A one-person Canadian brand started in 2009 out of dive-watch forums. The Seaforth gives you a double-domed sapphire crystal, a luminous sapphire bezel, titanium as an option and a 65-hour Swiss movement for $765. Owners have nothing bad to say about the watch itself. Getting one is the problem: batches sell out in minutes.",
"Trafford":"A small brand from Austin, Texas, started in 2020 by the designer Nathan Trafford. Its Crossroads is a square watch with a double-domed crystal, and the 2026 Crossroads S moved it to a Swiss hand-wound Sellita movement in a 9mm case for $899. Two-year warranty and 30-day returns. Colours come in seasons that sell out, though the Core blue and red stay in the range.",
"Circula":"A family firm in its third generation that has made watches in Pforzheim since 1955 and sells direct. Its Pro series hardens the case, the bracelet and the clasp to 1,200 Vickers, about six times harder than ordinary steel, and shields the movement from magnets up to 80,000 A/m, with a 65-hour Swiss movement, from $1,090. US orders add $99, which covers shipping and import duty. The catches: a two-year warranty, and delivery can take four to five weeks.",
"Archimede":"The watch brand of Ickler, a Pforzheim case maker, so the cases are made in its own workshop, and on the Protect models hardened to 1,200 Vickers. The OutDoor 39 Protect adds 200 m water resistance, a sapphire crystal and a 65-hour Swiss movement for about $1,300 before duty, with a two-year warranty. The catches: the bracelet is only hardened if you pay extra, the clasp has no hand adjustment, and it is sold direct from Germany.",
"Traska":"A Florida brand whose case and bracelet steel is hardened to almost 1,200 HV, about six times harder than ordinary steel, and one reviewer found no scratches after three years of testing. Its Japanese movements are regulated by the brand, with a box sapphire crystal, 100 m water resistance and a three-year warranty, for $750–950. Owners have almost no complaints beyond polished hands that can be hard to see.",
"Autodromo":"An industrial designer's watches modelled on car instruments: the Group B dial copies the tachometer of the Lancia 037 rally car, in a light two-part titanium case. Reviewers love the 1970s-80s styling and how slim it wears. The catches: only 50 m water resistance, the Group B is retired, and the current Monoposto ($895, 150 per colour) was sold out.",
"Seiko Prospex and Presage":"Seiko's own 70-hour movements, plus dial crafts rarely found at the price: the Presage enamel dials (HCC005, SPB495) are made by an enamel master and cost $1,400, while Urushi lacquer dials start at $1,650. The Prospex 1965 Heritage Diver (SPB143, $1,200) is a 200 m diver with a sapphire crystal, and owners say it runs better than its spec. The complaints: misaligned bezel inserts and thick cases.",
"Orient Star":"Orient's upper line since 1951, made by Seiko Epson, with Orient's own movements and its trademark gauge showing how much power is left. The newest F8 movements add a silicon escape wheel, which Orient Star is the only Japanese brand to use. Semi-skeleton models cost $470–740. Reviewers call it great value for an in-house movement with a complication; the complaints are busy dials and thick cases.",
"Tornek-Rayville":"The original was a Blancpain Fifty Fathoms renamed so the US Navy could buy it under the Buy American Act. Bill Yao of MKII revived it in 2021, and the TR-660 is a 200 m diver with a double-domed sapphire crystal for $949. Reviewers credit Yao's exacting fit and finish. It is thick at 14.7 mm, sold in pre-order batches with waits of about seven months, and the next TR-660 batch is due in 2027.",
"Sangin Instruments":"Started by a Marine Raider and his wife, and named after the Afghan town where he served. Its automatics have 68-hour Swiss La Joux-Perret movements, sapphire crystals and 200 m water resistance for $899. Owners report years of hard field use without failure. It is sold in a few runs a year with waitlists, and most models were sold out.",
"Marathon":"A family firm in its fourth generation that has made watches for the US military since 1941, built to the US military specification MIL-PRF-46374G and issued to US, Canadian and Israeli forces. Its tritium tubes glow for years without charging. Reviewers say it's built like a tank with Swiss-level fit and finish. Under $1,500: the 36 mm MSAR automatic ($1,400) and the quartz Navigator ($1,150). The complaints: thick, heavy and plain.",
"Christopher Ward":"Designed in England and hand-built in Biel, Switzerland, and one of the first brands to sell only online, since 2005. Its Bel Canto chiming watch won the Geneva watchmaking Grand Prix (GPHG) in 2023. Every watch has 60-day returns and a five-year movement warranty, and the 300 m Trident Pro costs $1,095. Owners praise finishing that compares with far dearer watches; the complaint is basic 38-hour Sellita movements.",
"CWC":"Founded in 1972 to keep supplying Britain's Ministry of Defence. In 1980 it issued the first quartz watch to British forces, the G10, and replaced the Rolex Submariner as the Royal Navy's diving watch. It is still Swiss-made to military specification with a three-year warranty. Owners value the real issue history and toughness. The complaints: fixed strap bars allow only pass-through straps, and the Royal Navy divers come only in 45 mm.",
"Paulin":"Founded in Glasgow in 2013 by three sisters and now owned by anOrdain. The watches are assembled in Glasgow, and the Neo's colourful dials are milled from aluminium and anodised in the UK, for $600. The Mara diver is 300 m with a 68-hour Swiss movement. Reviewers like the dials and typography; the Neo has no lume.",
"Isotope":"A small English brand that hides a teardrop motif in its crowns, hands and rotors, and has built its own jumping-hour module, the OVNI. The Hydrium is a 300 m Swiss automatic diver from $1,235, with blued screws and a decorated movement that reviewers say beat the price. The complaints: colours that don't quite match across dial, bezel and hands, and hard-to-read OVNI numerals.",
"Dekla":"A one-man Stuttgart workshop that builds each pilot watch to your choice of dial, hands, lume, crown and movement, making the cases, dials and heat-blued hands itself. The design follows the 1930s–40s German observation watch (B-Uhr). It uses Swiss ETA or Sellita movements, from €630. Reviewers call it strong value against Stowa; the complaints are small case-finishing flaws, and US buyers must go through WatchBuys.",
"Nivada Grenchen":"A Grenchen brand founded in 1926 whose 1950 Antarctic went with the US Navy to the South Pole. Its revivals are faithful to the 1960s originals. Under $1,500: the Antarctic ($945–995), Antarctic Diver ($1,050) and F77 ($1,260–1,460), with Swiss movements, plus quartz-based chronographs from $610. The mechanical Chronomaster starts at $1,975. Complaints: an unrefined chronograph movement and cheap optional bracelets.",
"Ollech & Wajs":"A Zurich brand from 1956 whose watches were bought by US soldiers in Vietnam and whose calculator-bezel Selectron was popular with NASA engineers. The relaunched tool watches use Swiss movements (the OW 8001 is COSC-certified) and the C-1000 is rated to 1,000 m in a 39.5 mm case. Note that prices are now CHF 1,596 and up, and the P-101 named here appears discontinued. Owners note long lugs and a cheap supplied strap.",
"Lorier":"Vintage proportions done carefully: the Neptune is a slim 39 mm, 200 m diver with sapphire crystal and bezel insert and a Japanese Miyota automatic. Owners who've worn one for years wish they'd bought sooner. Since the watch site Worn & Wound bought the brand in July 2026, every model costs $699–799 and is sold by pre-order.",
"Nodus":"Every watch is assembled, regulated and tested in the brand's own Los Angeles workshop and has a two-year warranty you can pass on. The Contrail GMT has a true travel movement whose local hour hand jumps on its own, and a clasp you can adjust on the wrist. Prices are now $525 and up, and the Contrail ($900) was sold out in every colour.",
"Oris":"Independent since 1904, not part of a group. Its own Calibre 400 runs five days, is rated −3/+5 seconds a day, keeps time after exposure to 2,250 gauss and has a 10-year warranty if you register the watch. Owners say the accuracy impresses. Note that Calibre 400 models start at $4,100, about Black Bay money; cheaper Oris use 41-hour Sellita movements. Servicing is slow.",
"Longines":"Every Spirit movement is a COSC-certified chronometer with a magnet-resistant silicon hairspring and a 72-hour reserve, and mechanical Longines carry a five-year warranty. Reviewers single out the case finishing and see it as the Swatch Group's value tier below Omega, much as Tudor sits under Rolex. Movements are made exclusively for Longines by ETA, its sister company. Some find the Spirit's lugs long.",
"Tudor":"Rolex's founder started Tudor, and it is still built next to a Rolex facility in Le Locle. Its main movements come from Kenissi, Tudor's own movement factory, and every one is a COSC chronometer; newer models such as the Black Bay 58 also pass METAS Master Chronometer testing (0 to +5 seconds a day). The T-fit clasp, fitted to several newer models but not the 41mm Black Bay GMT, adjusts by hand over 8 mm, and the five-year warranty is transferable. Reviewers find no cost-cutting in the finishing and praise the bracelets. Owners report the polished case sides scratch, early GMT date wheels failing, and slow warranty service.",
"Baume & Mercier":"In the guide for its one picked watch, not the whole brand. The Riviera Baumatic 10714 ($4,300) has a movement built for the brand by Richemont's ValFleurier that runs 120 hours, five days, with a silicon balance spring that resists magnets. Registering it extends the warranty to eight years. Reviewers note few dial and strap choices and expect discounts. Richemont sold the brand to Italy's Damiani Group in July 2026.",
"Fortis":"In the guide for its one picked watch, not the whole brand. The Marinemaster M-40 is a 300 m diver in a compact 40 mm case, with a hidden ring of lume inside the dial that reviewers single out, and a five-year warranty. Fortis is a family-owned Grenchen brand that used the Marinemaster name in 1954. The complaint: $3,200 is a lot for a modified Sellita movement with only 38 hours.",
"Formex":"A family-owned Biel company whose Essence ThirtyNine is a COSC chronometer with a 65-hour movement, for $1,850–1,980. Four springs between the inner and outer case absorb shocks, and the clasp lets out 3 or 6 mm on the wrist. Warranty is three years. Owners praise the build, dial and bracelet. Some dislike the exposed bezel screws, and the wide bracelet makes it wear large.",
"Nomos":"Nomos develops and makes its movements in its own workshops in Glashütte, Germany's watchmaking town, including the escapement, the part that regulates the watch. Prices start at $1,830 for the Club Campus, and the hand-wound cases are thin enough to slip under a shirt cuff. The complaints: accuracy out of the box, magnetisation, easily scratched cases and a two-year warranty.",
"Sinn":"A Frankfurt tool-watch maker known for engineering. It hardens case surfaces to about 1,200 Vickers against roughly 220 for ordinary steel, builds dive cases in submarine steel, uses drying capsules that stop fogging, and started the German pilot-watch standard DIN 8330. One owner found tegimented cases unmarked after three years. Note that the cheaper 556 ($1,590) and 104 have none of those technologies, and some find the design plain.",
"Damasko":"Damasko hardens its own steel all the way through, not just the surface, which it says makes it four times harder than normal steel, so cases resist dents as well as scratches. Several models carry its own A26 movement, adjusted in six positions, such as the DK36 at about $2,188. Owners report cases unmarked after years. The complaints: a heavy bracelet, designs that rarely change and weak lume.",
"Hanhart":"Hanhart made the 417 ES chronograph in 1954 for the West German Air Force, and Steve McQueen owned one. Today's 417 ES is hand-wound, with 58 hours, 100 m and a domed sapphire crystal, from $2,680; the column-wheel flyback version (press once to reset and restart the timer) costs $3,330. Reviewers rate it strong value against bigger Swiss brands. It is thick at 13.3 mm, and it is sold in the US only through WatchBuys.",
"King Seiko":"A revived 1960s Seiko line, with the sharp, faceted, mirror-polished cases of the 1965 KSK that reviewers single out. The KSK has a box sapphire crystal at 10.7 mm thick for $3,300, and the VANAC's movement is derived from Grand Seiko's and runs 72 hours. The complaints: it costs almost twice the earlier King Seikos for a similar look, and the clasp feels cheap.",
"Grand Seiko quartz":"Grand Seiko's 9F quartz is rated to ±10 seconds a year. It ages its own quartz crystals for three months, checks temperature 540 times a day, and drives full-size hands that land exactly on each marker. The cases are mirror-polished by hand (Zaratsu), with a five-year warranty, from $2,700. Owners complain the clasp has no fine adjustment and that most models have no lume.",
"The Citizen quartz":"Citizen's top line. The solar Caliber A060 is rated to ±5 seconds a year and runs about 18 months in the dark, with Tosa washi paper dials that let light through to the solar cell. The case is mirror-polished to Grand Seiko standards, reviewers say, and the warranty is five years. The catches: in the US it is sold only as limited editions at $2,995–4,125, and setting it needs a stylus.",
"Minase":"Kyowa, a Japanese cutting-tool maker that machined cases for other brands, makes fewer than 500 a year. The movement sits in an inner box, which also forms the dial, screwed into the outer case, and polishing one Divido takes up to 479 steps by hand. Reviewers call the bracelet among the best engineered at any price. The complaints: the movement is a finished Swiss ETA, prices run $5,150 and up, and delivery takes about three months.",
"Ming":"Won prizes at the Geneva watchmaking Grand Prix (GPHG) in 2019 and 2024, for watches designed in Kuala Lumpur and made in Switzerland. Owners and reviewers praise the build and how comfortable the cases and straps are. The complaints are the price for a modified Sellita movement, and small runs that sell out: recent models cost CHF 3,500 and up.",
"Oak & Oscar":"Designed in Chicago around Swiss Sellita movements, and every watch is assembled and checked by the brand's own watchmaker there. The clasp adjusts without tools and the dials are built in two layers. Reviewers praise the finishing; the mild complaint is that the simpler models sit in a crowded price bracket. Sold direct only, from $1,775.",
"Lorca":"A compact 36 mm travel watch with a second time zone, 200 m water resistance and a Swiss Soprod movement regulated to within 4 seconds a day, for $1,875. Owners praise the size and the bracelet. They complain about weak lume, a small crown and a 19 mm strap width that limits strap choice.",
"Anoma":"The triangular case with no lugs follows a 1950s table by the designer Charlotte Perriand, and reviewers welcome it as an original design, not another vintage remake. It has a Swiss Sellita movement and is assembled in Switzerland. The complaints: it feels thicker than its 9.45 mm, it is sold by pre-order, and the price has risen to £2,200.",
"Airain":"A faithful re-edition of the Type 20, the flyback chronograph Airain made for French military pilots in the 1950s, with a hand-wound column-wheel movement that runs 63 hours. Reviewers call a hand-wound flyback at this price outstanding value. It costs €3,450 with shipping and duties included.",
"Space One":"A jumping-hour display, usually found on far dearer watches, made by the Paris watchmaker Théo Auffret from nine parts added to a Swiss Soprod movement, from €1,500. Owners call it wearable art. Reviewers say it is hard to read and that the case, nearly 52 mm wide, doesn't suit every wrist. Every watch was sold out on the brand's shop when checked.",
"Vulcain":"Vulcain launched the Cricket in 1947, the first successful mechanical alarm wristwatch, and four US presidents owned one. Today's Cricket still uses Vulcain's own hand-wound alarm movement, and reviewers love the loud alarm. It starts at $4,640, which many find steep next to a vintage one; the Skindiver, without an alarm, starts at $1,790.",
"Aquastar":"Founded in 1962 by a diver to make diving instruments, and used by Jacques Cousteau. Mechanical watches returned in 2020 under Rick Marei, who earlier revived DOXA, with Swiss movements and prices from $1,290. Reviewers praise the faithful designs and the finishing; owners find the Deepstar chronograph thick and top-heavy.",
"Amida":"A revival of a 1976 driver's watch with no dial: a sapphire prism reflects the jumping hour and the minutes into a window on the side of the case, so it can be read without turning the wrist. It has a Swiss Soprod movement and costs from CHF 2,900. Reviewers admire how well it is made; some find it heavy at 15.6 mm thick, and dear next to a vintage original.",
"Kollokium":"Started by three watch-industry insiders, one of whom runs Louis Erard. The Projekt 01 dial is several hundred luminous pins set by hand, in a raw cast-steel case with a 68-hour Swiss movement, and reviewers call the price fair for the handwork. The catches: runs are small and sell out, the glow fades quickly, and Projekt 01 ended in September 2026.",
"Straum":"Two Norwegian industrial designers make dials textured after Arctic landscapes, in steel or titanium cases with a 68-hour Swiss movement, from $2,100. Every review singles out the dial. An early complaint, a clasp without fine adjustment, has been fixed; the hour and minute hands can be hard to tell apart.",
"Behrens":"A Shenzhen maker that designs and builds its own mechanical displays, such as the Rotary, which shows the time on two rotors modelled on a Wankel engine. It was nominated at the Geneva Grand Prix (GPHG) in 2020, 2025 and 2026. Reviewers see strong value against Swiss independents; the displays are slow to read. Eight models cost under $5,000, from $1,920.",
"Celadon":"Chinese crafts done by named masters: the Century line has cloisonné enamel dials fired by hand in Beijing and hand-finished movements by the watchmaker Lin Yong Hua, and reviewers praise both. Be aware that the Century costs $18,900 and up. The watches under $5,000 have stamped dials and Chinese factory movements, from about S$1,888.",
"anOrdain":"A Glasgow workshop that makes its own vitreous enamel dials by hand, something otherwise found on watches costing many times more, with a five-year warranty that includes a free service. Owners rave about the dials. Be aware that prices have risen (the Model 2 is from about $2,666), it is sold through a paid waiting list of up to two years, and it is only rated to 50 m.",
"Atelier Wen":"The dials are hand-turned on a rose engine (a guilloché lathe) by the workshop of Cheng Yucai, China's first master of the craft. The current Perception has a French 65-hour movement, a clasp you adjust with a button and a five-year warranty. Reviewers rate it against anything at the price, but the price has roughly doubled since launch to $4,850.",
"Kurono Tokyo":"Each design is sketched and prototyped by the independent watchmaker Hajime Asaoka, whose own watches cost far more, and made in Japan with a box sapphire crystal. Owners buy it for the dials. The catches: it is sold only in short online order windows that sell out, the movement is an inexpensive Miyota, 2026 models start at $1,850, and the warranty is one year.",
"Omega":"Every Master Chronometer is tested for 10 days by METAS, the Swiss federal metrology institute. That means 0 to +5 seconds a day, and it keeps working next to a 15,000-gauss magnet, far stronger than anything in daily life. It uses George Daniels's Co-Axial escapement and comes with a five-year warranty. Owners report accuracy inside spec years on. The complaints are repeated price rises (a steel Aqua Terra is now $6,700–7,400) and Aqua Terra bracelets without fine adjustment.",
"Cartier":"Cartier designed the Santos in 1904 for the aviator Alberto Santos-Dumont, one of the first pilot's watches, and the Tank in 1917; both are still made. Today's Santos has Cartier's own movement, and you can swap the bracelet and remove links by hand without tools. Owners love the comfort. Reviewers note the movement's 42 hours and −3/+7 seconds a day trail Rolex and Omega at $8,400.",
"Zenith":"The El Primero, unveiled in 1969, beats 36,000 times an hour, fast enough to time tenths of a second. In the Chronomaster Sport the stopwatch hand laps the dial every 10 seconds. It was good enough that Rolex used it in the Daytona for over a decade. Owners rate the design and accuracy highly; price is the main complaint, at $11,200–12,300.",
"Jaeger-LeCoultre":"It has made over 1,200 movements and supplied Patek Philippe, Vacheron Constantin and Audemars Piguet. Every watch is tested for 1,000 hours after it is cased, and registering it extends the warranty to eight years. The Reverso's flip-over case dates from 1931 and starts at $5,900 in steel. The complaints: steep price rises, and only 30 m water resistance.",
"Glashütte Original":"More than 95% of each watch is made by the brand itself, including the dials, now made in Glashütte. Its Calibre 36 runs 100 hours and is tested for 24 days; note that the Seventies Chronograph named here has a different, 70-hour movement. Reviewers say the finishing is far above typical sports watches. Owners' complaint is weak resale value.",
"Grand Seiko mechanical":"The Snowflake's Spring Drive is wound by a spring like a mechanical watch but regulated by quartz, so it keeps to about a second a day and the seconds hand glides instead of ticking. The cases are mirror-polished by hand (Zaratsu), the warranty is five years, and the Snowflake's titanium is about 30% lighter than steel. Owners complain that most bracelets can't be fine-adjusted and that resale is weak. The ivory SBGM221 travel watch uses a mechanical 9S movement instead, regulated to +5 to -3 seconds a day.",
"The Citizen mechanical":"Caliber 0200 was developed with Citizen's Swiss sister company La Joux-Perret and is rated to −3/+5 seconds a day, with a 60-hour reserve and a five-year warranty. Reviewers rate the case and bracelet finishing at Grand Seiko level. The catches: the clasp has no fine adjustment, $7,000–8,400 is a lot with 'Citizen' on the dial, and the 0200 itself was sold out in the US.",
"Horage":"Horage designs its own movements in Biel and regulates them to chronometer level (−4/+6 seconds a day). They include a 3.6 mm micro-rotor that keeps the Supersede under 10 mm thick, and tourbillons from CHF 8,999, rare at that price. Prices include US duties. Reviewers note the movement decoration is industrial rather than hand-finished, the warranty is two years, and new models can take two years to arrive.",
"Louis Erard":"An independent Swiss brand from 1929 that works with famous independent watchmakers such as Alain Silberstein, Vianney Halter, Konstantin Chaykin and Kudoke on regulator watches, which show hours, minutes and seconds on separate dials. They cost about CHF 3,000–4,500, a fraction of those makers' own watches. The trade-off is a plain, bought-in Sellita movement and a thick 12.8 mm case. Warranty is three years.",
"Sartory Billard":"A French industrial designer's brand built around bespoke watches, finished and assembled in France: you choose the dial, hands and colours with the maker. The SB04-E uses a 68-hour Swiss La Joux-Perret movement, from €3,500. Reviewers say the dials and hands are where the money goes, not the ordinary movement; bespoke orders take about five months and need a 50% deposit.",
"Habring²":"Richard Habring designed IWC's simplified split-seconds chronograph in 1992; he and his wife Maria now make about 200 watches a year in Austria. The Felix has their own hand-wound A11 movement, regulated to chronometer limits, and won a Geneva Grand Prix (GPHG) prize in 2015, one of four for the brand. Collectors love dealing with the couple directly; the movement finishing is plain, and custom orders take months.",
"Kudoke":"Stefan Kudoke, a master watchmaker trained in Glashütte, finishes every movement by hand and engraves its balance cock by hand. The Kudoke 2 won the GPHG Petite Aiguille in 2019, and the Kudoke 3 in 2024. It costs from €10,701 before tax, about $13,290 at a US retailer, cheap for this much handwork. Note the movement is developed from a Habring² design, the watches aren't thin, and deliveries take months.",
"Lundis Bleus":"A tiny Swiss workshop, now run by one of its two founders, that fires its own enamel dials, including grand feu, the hardest kind, and makes every watch to order. The Ref. 1120 costs from CHF 3,500 before tax with a Swiss Sellita movement and a five-year warranty. Owners buy it for the dials; the movement is bought in, and you pay in full up front.",
"Laine":"Torsti Laine, a Finnish watchmaker who worked with A. Lange & Söhne and Kari Voutilainen, hand-turns the guilloché dials and hand-polishes the hands in La Chaux-de-Fonds. He won the Lange Watchmaking Excellence Award in 2014. The V38 is CHF 12,500 before tax. Note that its micro-rotor movement is finished by its maker, Vaucher, not by Laine, and prices have risen since 2021.",
"Dornblüth & Sohn":"A nine-person family workshop in Kalbe, Germany, that makes about 80% of each watch itself, including every dial and hand, and builds each one to order, a few a month. The 99.1 starts at $7,170 from the US importer, on a reworked Unitas base. The fully in-house Quintus line costs more. Owners rate the finishing outstanding; the complaint is waits of nine months to two years.",
"Felipe Pikullik":"A young Berlin watchmaker trained under Stefan Kudoke, who hand-skeletonises, frosts and bevels movements. The entry Klassik ($9,500) uses a reworked ETA 6498, and the newest models carry his own movements. Owners praise the hand bevelling. Be aware that most of the range costs $17,600–47,000, and an owner found the engraving shallow.",
"Otsuka Lotec":"Jiro Katayama, a former car designer who taught himself watchmaking on a lathe bought at auction, builds his own displays on Japanese Miyota movements, such as a jumping hour or two retrograde hands. He won GPHG prizes in 2024 and 2026, at ¥396,000–1,089,000 (about $2,500–6,800). The big catch: they are sold only by lottery to buyers with a Japanese address and card, with a one-year warranty.",
"Breitling":"Breitling's Navitimer, designed in 1952, put a slide-rule bezel on a pilot's chronograph, and Sinn's 903 is built on the same design. Today's B01 movement is Breitling's own, with a 70-hour reserve and a five-year warranty. Its value rating is low because new prices are high for what you get: grey dealers sell a new Navitimer B01 41 for about a fifth less than list.",
"Jack Mason":"A small Texas brand that assembles and regulates its watches in the USA and sells them direct with a five-year warranty. Its Strat-o-timer GMT has a true travel movement, whose local hour hand jumps on its own, plus a clasp you can adjust on the wrist, for about $1,100. Reviewers say the finishing is only fair and the clasp is long.",
};
