"tudor-black-bay-pro-opaline":"Chosen as the Tudor travel watch with a light dial and a clasp that adjusts by hand: at 39 mm it is smaller than the Black Bay GMT's 41 mm, and its T-fit clasp adjusts on the wrist, which the GMT's does not. Tudor's own Kenissi GMT movement is a COSC chronometer that runs about 70 hours, and the warranty is five years and transferable. Note that its 24-hour bezel is fixed, so it can't follow a third time zone the way the Black Bay GMT's turning bezel can, it is COSC only, not METAS, at 14.6 mm it is thick, and owners have reported date-wheel faults on early examples of this movement.",
"tudor-black-bay-gmt-opaline":"Chosen as the Tudor travel watch with the opaline dial and a 24-hour bezel that turns, so it can follow three time zones, which the Black Bay Pro's fixed bezel can't. The Black Bay 58 GMT also has a turning bezel and is smaller and slimmer, but only comes in black. Tudor's own Kenissi GMT movement is a COSC chronometer that runs about 70 hours, and the warranty is five years and transferable. Note that the clasp has no adjustment by hand (reviewers point out it lacks the T-fit clasp the Pro has), at 14.6 mm thick with flat case sides and about 50 mm lug to lug it wears large, and it is COSC only, not METAS. Early examples of this movement had date-wheel faults; these were widely reported as fixed during 2020, before this dial arrived in 2023, though Tudor has never confirmed it.",
